    "question": "What is the difference between a traditional IRA and a Roth IRA in terms of tax treatment?",
    "reference": "Traditional IRA contributions may be tax-deductible (reducing taxable income in the contribution year), but withdrawals in retirement are taxed as ordinary income. Roth IRA contributions are made with after-tax dollars (no upfront deduction), but qualified withdrawals in retirement are completely tax-free.",
    "candidate": "## Traditional IRA vs. Roth IRA: Tax Treatment\n\nThe two account types differ primarily in *when* your money is taxed. With a traditional IRA, you may be able to deduct some or all of your contributions depending on your circumstances, providing a potential upfront tax benefit [1][3]. Additionally, amounts in a traditional IRA, including earnings and gains, are generally not taxed until they are distributed [3]. This means you defer taxes now but will owe them when you withdraw funds in retirement.\n\nA Roth IRA works in the opposite direction. Contributions to a Roth IRA are made with after-tax dollars, but qualified distributions \u2014 including earnings \u2014 are tax-free [2]. You also do not include in your gross income distributions from a Roth IRA that you roll over tax-free into another Roth IRA [2]. An additional advantage of the Roth IRA is flexibility: if you choose, you can leave amounts in your Roth IRA as long as you live, with no required minimum distributions during your lifetime [5].\n\n**Key tradeoff:** The traditional IRA offers a potential tax deduction today but taxes you on withdrawal, while the Roth IRA offers no upfront deduction but provides tax-free growth and distributions \u2014 making the better choice dependent on your current vs. expected future tax rate.",
